        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What is the occupation of Manuel da Cruz?\n\nAnswer A: a man of the cloth\n  about: Manuel da Cruz\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Manuel da Cruz, a man of the cloth, made his way through the winding streets of the small village.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Catholic priest\n  about: Manuel da Cruz\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Da Cruz was ordained as a Catholic priest in the early 18th century. As a priest, he was responsible for administering the sacraments, preaching, and providing spiritual guidance to his congregation.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As the sun set over the rolling hills of rural France, Manuel da Cruz, a man of the cloth, made his way through the winding streets of the small village. His worn, black boots clicked against the cobblestone pavement, a rhythmic accompaniment to the soft rustling of his priestly robes. The villagers, familiar with the Portuguese prelate's gentle nature, nodded in respect as he passed, their faces creasing into warm smiles. Manuel's duties as a priest often took him beyond the confines of the village church, into the surrounding countryside. Today, he was bound for the Château de la Roche, where the local lord, Monsieur le Comte, had requested his presence. The Comte, a just and fair man, was known for his generosity towards the villagers, and Manuel had grown fond of him over the years. As he approached the château, Manuel noticed a group of men gathered near the entrance, their faces etched with concern. They were the anciens cadres, a socio-professional category of experienced professionals who had served the Comte and his family for generations. Manuel recognized several of them, including Monsieur Dupont, the Comte's trusted advisor, and Monsieur LaFleur, the château's master gardener. The men parted as Manuel approached, their eyes flicking towards him with a mixture of curiosity and respect. \"Bonjour, Monsieur le Prêtre,\" Monsieur Dupont said, his voice low and gravelly. \"The Comte awaits you. We have... a situation, of sorts.\" Manuel's curiosity was piqued, but he merely nodded and followed the men into the château. As they walked, he couldn't help but notice the way the anciens cadres moved with a quiet confidence, their years of service and experience evident in every step. It was a quality he admired, one that he, too, had cultivated over the years. In the grand hall, the Comte sat behind a large, oak desk, his face lined with worry. \"Manuel, my friend,\" he said, his voice laced with urgency. \"I fear we have a problem on our hands. One of our villagers, a young woman named Sophie, has gone missing. Her family is frantic, and I fear the worst.\" Manuel's eyes met those of the anciens cadres, and he sensed a subtle understanding pass between them.\nDocument 2: **Manuel da Cruz**\n\nManuel da Cruz (1690-1764) was a Portuguese prelate who served as a Catholic priest. **Early Life and Education**\n\nManuel da Cruz was born in 1690 in Portugal. Little is known about his early life, but it is believed that he received his education in theology and philosophy at a seminary in Portugal. **Priesthood**\n\nDa Cruz was ordained as a Catholic priest in the early 18th century. As a priest, he was responsible for administering the sacraments, preaching, and providing spiritual guidance to his congregation. **Evidence of Priesthood**\n\nSeveral sources confirm that Manuel da Cruz worked as a Catholic priest. According to the Portuguese National Archives, da Cruz was listed as a priest in the Diocese of Lisbon in 1720. [1] Additionally, a biography of da Cruz published in 1750 by the Portuguese historian, António Carvalho da Costa, describes him as a \"devout and dedicated priest\" who served in several parishes throughout Portugal. [2]\n\n**Career**\n\nDa Cruz's career as a priest spanned several decades, during which he served in various capacities. He was known for his piety and his commitment to his congregation. In 1740, he was appointed as the vicar of the parish of São Pedro in Lisbon, a position he held until his death in 1764. [3]\n\n**Legacy**\n\nManuel da Cruz's legacy as a Catholic priest is still remembered in Portugal today. He is revered for his dedication to his faith and his service to his community. **References**\n\n[1] Portuguese National Archives. (1720). Diocese of Lisbon: List of Priests. [2] Carvalho da Costa, A. (1750). Vidas dos Bispos e Padres Portugueses. Lisbon: Officina de Miguel Rodrigues. [3] Diocese of Lisbon. (1740). Appointment of Manuel da Cruz as Vicar of São Pedro Parish. Note: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
